[Music]  most americans are aware of the roles  that rosa parks martin luther king jr  bus boycotts and school desegregation  played in the american civil rights  movement not as well known are the  effects of the constitution's commerce  clause and partisan politics  hi i'm rich burnett for one dream in  this episode of perspectives we'll hear  from four experts on government history  and constitutional law as they explain  some lesser-known moments that shape the  civil rights movement in the united  states  we start now by looking at the period  after the american civil war that led to  the call for civil rights in america  [Music]  during the era of jim crow in the  american south which lasts from the late  1870s until the mid-1960s  americans experienced extreme  discrimination and considerable violence  in this roughly 80-year period the  injustices and inequalities of everyday  life for african-americans in the south  was brutal  with lynchings a common occurrence  a critical juncture occurred in 1896  when the supreme court decided the  famous case of plessy versus ferguson  in this case the supreme court ruled  that separate railway cars for whites  and blacks was permissible  as long as the cars were equal in  facilities  citing this separate but equal clause  states and cities instituted laws that  prevented direct contact between blacks  and whites  segregating lunch counters bus station  waiting rooms drinking fountains and  even bathrooms  these segregation laws and efforts to  promote racial discrimination were  largely aimed at dehumanizing african  americans  so even though slavery had officially  ended  african americans were prevented from  owning property escaping poverty  attaining education and training and  participating as equal citizens in  american democracy  the ruling of plessy versus ferguson  ensured the survival of jim crow laws  for over a half century  we credit the american civil rights  movement as helping to significantly  expand democracy and civil rights in  america  the movement is a profound example of a  successful social movement that aimed to  change laws and attitudes for the direct  benefit of african americans and the  collective benefit of all americans  in 1954 the supreme court decided the  famous case of brown versus board of  education of topeka kansas  overturning the precedent of separate  but equal that was set in the plessy  case some 60 years earlier  in brown vs board chief justice earl  warren wrote the opinion of the court  calling for an end to legalized racial  segregation in public schools  this was a huge victory for the civil  rights cause  but the decision led also to massive  backlash  u.s senate and house members from the  south  vowed to prevent enforcement with new  legislation  and membership in the racist ku klux  klan organization spiked  against this backdrop a new boycott  began  on december 1st 1955  rosa parks was taking the bus home from  her job as a department store seamstress  in montgomery alabama when a white man  boarded and the bus driver ordered her  to give up her seat  rosa parks and her husband had served in  various roles at the local chapter of  the naacp and had investigated sexual  assault cases during the 1940s  now  she refused to relinquish her seat on  the full bus  she was arrested and taken into custody  outraging the local black community  african-american leaders including a  young minister new to town by the name  of martin luther king jr  gathered in the mount zion church to  coordinate a response  they decided to boycott city buses on  the day of rosa parks's trial on  december 5th 1955.  the campaign drew such overwhelming  support  that black leaders extended the boycott  for  381 days  dr king argued that it was an effective  method of passive resistance he said  christ showed us the way  and gandhi in india showed it could work  president john f kennedy moved  cautiously on civil rights during the  first two years of his administration  but in 1963 he committed to the cause  kennedy said the events in birmingham  have so increased the cries for equality  that they can no longer be ignored  in a televised address that june he said  this nation  for all its hopes and all its boast  will not be fully free  until all its citizens are free  and he said he would send civil rights  legislation to congress  banning racial segregation  throughout the country  at the time  activists had been planning a massive  protest march in washington  now it would be in support of the  proposed civil rights law  on august 28 1963  some 250 000 people  took part in the march  and martin luther king delivered his  famous i have a dream speech  100 years after abraham lincoln's famous  emancipation proclamation  african americans still weren't free  king said  instead  they lived as exiles in their own land  as president kennedy's civil rights  legislation made its way to congress  it might have appeared that the civil  rights movement had reached a turning  point  but in birmingham  segregationists bombed the city's 16th  street baptist church on september  15th four young girls  getting ready for the service  died in the blast  two months later  kennedy himself was assassinated  vice president lyndon johnson assumed  the highest office in the land  and publicly committed to seeing  kennedy's civil rights legislation  through  but the problem of segregation is  relevant to our continuing exploration  of the commerce clause because the  court's rulings in brown and other  important equal protection cases  actually changed little for african  americans on the ground for many years  it was to address this situation that a  full decade after brown congress passed  the civil rights act of 1964  and it was the commerce clause that the  government used to justify many parts of  that statute  you may well ask why  a statute about civil rights certainly  seems like it has more to do with  concepts like equal protection and basic  fairness than with the sort of economic  matters that would seem to be at the  core of the commerce clause  a more plausible constitutional  provision to justify the civil rights  act might therefore be section 5 of the  14th amendment which empowers congress  to pass laws enforcing the substantive  provisions of the 14th amendment  including the equal protection clause  when congress sees violations of the  14th amendment it can legislate under  section 5 to try to prevent or remedy  those violations  the problem with using section 5 to  support the civil rights act however was  that some important 19th century  precedent had construed that section  narrowly  specifically in an 1883 case called the  civil rights cases the court had held  that congress can only legislate under  section 5 to address governmental  violations of rights  but segregation was not just a product  of governmental decision  to be sure there was state-sponsored  segregation and congress pretty clearly  could use section 5 to pass a law  addressing that  but many private white businesses also  practice segregation denying african  americans a wide range of services  at businesses such as restaurants  theaters and hotels  many provisions of the civil rights act  of 1964 dealt with this private racism  and exclusion  the civil rights act signed into law in  july of 1964  prohibited discrimination in public  places  made employment discrimination illegal  and provided for the integration of  schools and other public facilities  it was the most significant civil rights  legislation since the civil war era  unlike revolutionaries in other  historical contexts  civil rights activists didn't pick up  arms or man barricades  instead they joined arms sang hymns such  as we shall overcome  and use their bodies and courage to  assert the rights of all people  and the justice of their cause  a segregationist attacked with billy  clubs nooses  guns and bombs  civil rights revolutionaries  wielded what dr king called  the weapon of love  this  in and of itself was revolutionary  on august 6th 1965  congress enacted another piece of  significant civil rights legislation the  voting rights act  it stipulated that no state or local  government could deny american citizens  the right to vote on the basis of race  in the months after passage  250 000 new black voters registered to  exercise rights originally granted to  them in the 15th amendment  it was in the fifth party system that  democratic candidate for president john  f kennedy calculated that if democrats  embraced the enfranchisement of african  americans in the south  the party could expand its coalition in  a way that would provide an advantage to  northern liberals such as kennedy  it's important to remember that during  this time  ideology and political party were not as  aligned as they are today  the democratic coalition was made up of  northerners and conservative southerners  and the republican coalition was made up  of conservative northerners  in retrospect it seems like the  republican party could have moved first  to enfranchise african americans and  expanded their voting coalition in the  south but the democrats beat them to it  it was under a democratic president that  the civil rights act and voting rights  act were passed in 1964 and 1965  setting up a virtual marriage of  political loyalty between democrats and  african americans that still persists  today  but passage of the 1965 voting rights  act  and the martyrdom of martin luther king  jr in 1968 did not secure  racial justice in this country  on the contrary the escalating mass  incarceration of black men in our  nation's prisons over the last 50 years  now exists  as a vehicle of social control  on a scale that actually exceeds that of  19th century slavery  more black men are currently under  custodial control  in prisons on probation or on parole  than were held as slaves in 1850  at the height of the plantation system  all of this is recent history the result  of a backlash against advances made by  african americans during the civil  rights movement  beginning with barry goldwater's tough  on crime presidential campaign in 1964  and the shift to focusing on street  crime and drug law enforcement that  accompanied president reagan's election  america's half-century-old war on drugs  has swept more and more black people  into prison  incarcerating them for petty drug  offences  for which they were and still sometimes  are disproportionately punished when  compared to white defendants committing  similar crimes  hey thanks for watching if you'd like to  learn more about the topics in this  episode the full list of series that  these clips came from is in the  description below you can watch them all  on one dream and don't forget to  subscribe to this youtube channel for  new episodes of perspectives and you can  watch previous ones here

